import { links } from '../links';
---

<section class="band" id="problem">
  <div class="wrap split reveal">
    <div>
      <p class="label">The problem</p>
      <h2>Every validator does everything.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Most layer 1 blockchains make you pick two of throughput, decentralization, and
        state. Ethereum keeps decentralization, but every validator stores and processes
        the whole state, which caps throughput. Solana reaches high throughput through a
        leader-based pipeline, where the leader is the bottleneck and the hardware bar
        decides who gets to take part. Sui parallelizes execution with an object model and
        still has every validator process every transaction.
      </p>
      <p>
        The usual way out is to trust fewer validators or push the work to a layer 2.
        BluePods does neither. Each object has a defined set of <strong>holder
        validators</strong> that store it, attest to it, and execute the transactions that
        touch it. A transfer involves the ten to fifty holders of the objects in play, not
        the entire network.
      </p>
      <p>
        State and execution are sharded. The order everything passes through is not.
      </p>
      <a class="jump" href={links.vision} rel="noopener">Read the vision &#8627;</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .prose .jump {
    justify-self: start;
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }
</style>
